WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:05.731
I remember being seven years old, living in
Iran, and my father would take me to one of

00:00:05.731 --> 00:00:06.731
my favorite places.

00:00:06.731 --> 00:00:09.110
It was an indoor swimming pool.

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:13.630
Massive indoor swimming pool And they would
make these special drinks that I loved.

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:17.790
And there was a man there who was a good friend
of the family.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:20.210
They now live in Southern California.

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:21.710
Love their family.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:26.579
And I remember that every time I would speak
to this man, he would always give me so much

00:00:26.579 --> 00:00:27.579
respect.

00:00:27.579 --> 00:00:28.580
Always give me so much respect.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:34.480
And on time he sat me down and he looked me
in the eye and he told me, "Patrick, do you

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.760
know why I respect you so much?"

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.469
And I said, "No, I really don't know."

00:00:39.469 --> 00:00:44.739
He said, "Because one day, you're going to
lead the world.

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:50.609
Because one day, you, as a seven-year-old,
are going to be a 25, 35, 45-year old leading

00:00:50.609 --> 00:00:51.629
other people.

00:00:51.629 --> 00:00:53.850
I want to give you respect from now."

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:57.129
I get the chills every time I tell this story,
all over my body.

00:00:57.129 --> 00:01:03.700
You see, because, any president in the world,
any great CEO in the world, any great entrepreneur

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:09.680
in the world, at one point was a teenager
with a dream and a heartbeat, thinking, I

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.880
want to do something big in my life.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.750
I want to make an impact in my life.

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:14.750
How do I do it?

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:19.770
So today's message is directed to teenage
entrepreneurs.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:24.000
And by the way, if you're watching this and
you're not a teenage entrepreneur, share this

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.361
video with teenage entrepreneurs that you
may know, or even teenagers that want to figure

00:01:28.361 --> 00:01:29.600
out a way to make more money.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.810
I'm going to share 14 different ideas that
any teenager can put in place, and start making

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:35.810
money today.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:40.920
Because I'm talking from experience on how
I started making money at ten years old.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:46.369
Let me start off telling you a couple of ways
that I did it.

00:01:46.369 --> 00:01:51.320
I remember being ten years old running a recycling
business at a swimming pool, local in Germany.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.299
I remember being 13 years old, going around
all over the place.

00:01:55.299 --> 00:01:57.859
I had a point here, so I want to go with the
first one.

00:01:57.859 --> 00:02:01.229
The first point for teen age entrepreneurs
to make money -- it's something I did.

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:05.680
And I'm telling you, I didn't grow up in a
wealthy family.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.580
Some people that are watching this may say,
"I'm from a prestigious family, I would never

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.910
bring myself to that level."

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:12.910
That's totally fine.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:13.910
I'm just telling you how I made my money.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:18.190
I was 13 years old, living in Glendale, California
and I had a friend of mine named Adrian.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:24.750
We wanted to buy this card of Sergei Fedorov
and Sergei Fedorov at that time Upper Deck

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:25.750
came out.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.910
Some of you may know Sergei Fedorov, their
rookie card was a fascinating rookie card.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:35.400
We both wanted it and we wanted to buy a box
of Upper Deck and split the 36 packs, 18 and

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.480
18 we'd split it.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.330
And so when our parents would go to sleep
at night at 10, 11 o'clock, we had a shopping

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:42.330
cart.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:45.610
I'd go grab the shopping cart and we'd go
to all the local -- this is what I did -- you

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:46.610
don't have to do this.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:54.470
We'd go to the local trashcans and we'd collect
either the cans or two liter bottles.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:58.700
and we would collect them, we'd go to the
local Albertson's and recycle every single

00:02:58.700 --> 00:02:59.700
one of them.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.040
We'd make five bucks, and we'd do that a few
times a week.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.120
Eventually after a month, you have about $100.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:05.260
And you can buy cards.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.980
Whatever it was, that was mine.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.090
Today, there are many other ways to do this.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:15.780
If I was 12 years old today, 11 years old
today, 13 years old today, I would go to every

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:19.943
single person in my community that my parents
approved of -- you've got to make sure your

00:03:19.943 --> 00:03:24.000
parents can say that you can talk to these
guys, and you've got to make sure you get

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.370
the confirmation from your parents.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:33.010
But I would go and I would ask, hey, do you
mind if I collect all your cans and I would

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:38.190
give them special plastic bags or boxes for
them to put and you'd come and clean it for

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:39.980
them, except you're recycling.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:43.860
And I would bring that back, and have bags
and ask my mom or my dad to take me to the

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:51.060
recycling place and I would recycle and take
that money, so one, you're being a positive,

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:54.530
great citizen to society because you're cleaning
the place out and number two, you're making

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:55.980
money for doing that.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:04:00.320
That's one of the ways that any, any, any
teenage entrepreneur can make money.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.330
You may say, "Pat, I don't like that idea."

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:03.330
Stay with me.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:04.330
Second idea.

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:07.130
Here's the second idea.

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:08.280
Social media.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.490
Most adults have no clue about social media.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:12.530
I tell you no clue.

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:17.509
When I say adults, I'm not talking about 22-year-olds,
I'm taking about maybe 35, 45.

00:04:17.509 --> 00:04:21.090
It's not that they don't have any clue -- but
most of them don't know a lot about social

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:22.090
media.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:24.280
And you would be amazed how many of them are
intimidated by it.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:30.290
For instance, this whole idea came about because
I'm on Snapchat and I'm pretty active now

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:31.290
